  "source": "Cremer 3e Table 4.3, PDF page 201 (printed p. 191)",
  "about": "Thirteen metals at 20 C, in the order the page prints them, from section 4.5 \"Experimental Data\". It is the one table in this catalogue that prints the modulus, the shear modulus and the Poisson ratio together with both speeds, so each row checks three independent relations at once. Its speed column is c_LII, which Cremer defines in Eq. (3.32) as sqrt(E/rho), the quasi-longitudinal speed on a rod: the Roman subscript counts the directions along which the cross-sectional contraction is unconstrained, so II is a bar and not a plate. Lead and copper each print two specimens under one name, which differ only in the flexural loss factor, so they are two rows here. Section 4.5.1 says the loss factor of a metal \"cannot be considered as a material constant\", because cold rolling, heat treatment and irradiation move it; the moduli and the Poisson ratio it calls relatively independent of load history and frequency. The bracketed references the Remarks column carries are resolved to their authors from the chapter's own list, PDF page 243 (printed p. 233).",
